course: biology
lesson: 14
title: The city as a habitat
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To a wild animal a city is not concrete and cars, it is a strange new landscape: tall buildings are cliffs, railway banks are hedges, gardens are meadows, and bins are a free buffet.
explain: Pigeons nest on station ledges as their rock dove ancestors nested on sea cliffs, peregrine falcons nest on cathedral towers and dive on those pigeons, and foxes trot down quiet streets at night.
explain: The animals that win in cities are the clever, bold, eat-almost-anything kind: crows, gulls, rats, foxes and raccoons learn fast and try new things.
explain: City life changes animals too: robins sing under streetlamps at night, and some great tits sing higher to be heard over the traffic.
explain: City animals are wild animals, so watch them, but do not feed, chase or touch them.
fact: City pigeons are descended from wild rock doves that nest on sea cliffs, and ledges on tall buildings are like cliffs to them; a war pigeon called Cher Ami carried a message that helped save nearly 200 soldiers in 1918.
fact: Red foxes live in many cities, like London and Bristol, eating worms, rats, fruit and scraps, with dens in gardens, parks and railway banks.
fact: Peregrine falcons nest on tall buildings like the cliffs they use in the wild and hunt city pigeons; a diving peregrine can go faster than 300 kilometres per hour, the fastest animal on Earth.
fact: Some city robins sing at night under streetlamps, because the light tricks them and night is quieter; great tits in noisy cities sing higher notes than forest great tits.
fact: Dandelion seeds float on tiny parachutes into pavement cracks, where dust and water collect, and a long taproot finds water deep down.
fact: Brown rats eat almost anything, have many babies and can squeeze through holes about the size of a coin; the saying that you are never more than 2 metres from a rat is a myth, and rats can carry disease, so never touch them.
fact: Herring gulls learn that people carry food and swoop to grab it; scientists in England found gulls were slower to grab food when a person stared straight at them.
fact: Small bats like pipistrelles hunt insects around street lamps by echolocation, shouting and listening for echoes; bats are not blind, and one pipistrelle can eat thousands of midges in a night.
fact: Utrecht in the Netherlands planted sedum and flowers on hundreds of bus stop roofs to feed bees, soak up rain and cool the city a little.
fact: Carrion crows in Sendai, Japan, were filmed putting nuts on the road for cars to crack and picking up the pieces at the red light; crows can recognise human faces for years.
fact: House sparrows have nested in holes in buildings beside people for thousands of years, but they have become much rarer in some cities like London, and scientists are still working out why.
fact: Raccoons open bins with nimble, hand-like front paws; Toronto made raccoon-proof bins, and some raccoons still learned to open them.
exercise: Why do pigeons nest on station ledges? | Their wild ancestors nested on sea cliffs, and ledges are like cliffs | They were trained by station staff long ago to sit there | Ledges are the only warm places in cities | Pigeons cannot build nests in trees
exercise: What did the pigeon Cher Ami do in 1918? | Carried a message that helped save nearly 200 soldiers | Flew all the way around the world without stopping once | Found a lost king in a forest | Won the first pigeon race in London
exercise: Where do city foxes make their dens? | In gardens, parks and railway banks | On the flat roofs of tall office buildings | Inside shops, hiding under the counters | Only in zoos, behind tall wire fences
exercise: Why do peregrine falcons nest on cathedral towers? | The towers are like cliffs, and the city is full of pigeons | Church bells keep other birds away from the nest | The stone is warm from the heating | They like the sound of the organ and the choir
exercise: What is the fastest animal on Earth? | A diving peregrine falcon, over 300 kilometres per hour | A cheetah sprinting across the open plains after a gazelle | A sailfish in the ocean | A racing pigeon flying home
exercise: Why does a robin sing at night under a streetlamp? | The light tricks it, and night is quieter than the daytime traffic | Robins are owls in disguise | It is scared of the dark and sings to feel brave | It has forgotten where its nest is
exercise: How do great tits in noisy cities change their song? | They sing higher notes to be heard over the traffic hum | They stop singing completely | They sing only in winter | They sing much lower, like the hum of a car engine
exercise: How does a dandelion reach a crack in the pavement? | Its seed floats there on a little parachute | A bird plants it there on purpose | Its roots crawl under the road from a nearby park | It grows from the cement itself
exercise: How small a hole can a brown rat squeeze through? | About the size of a coin | Only a hole as big as a football | Only an open door or window | Rats cannot squeeze through anything at all
exercise: Is it true that you are never more than 2 metres from a rat? | No, it is a myth with no real count behind it | Yes, scientists have counted every rat in the country | Yes, but only in London | No, you are never more than 2 kilometres
exercise: What made gulls slower to grab someone's chips? | The person staring straight at them | The person wearing a bright red sun hat | The person eating the chips very fast | Nothing, gulls are never slowed down
exercise: How does a pipistrelle find midges in the dark? | Echolocation: it shouts and listens for echoes | It smells them from a kilometre away | It follows the light of the street lamps and sees the midges glowing | It is blind and just bumps into them
exercise: Why did Utrecht plant flowers on bus stop roofs? | To feed bees, soak up rain and cool the city | To hide the roofs from the pigeons | To grow fresh vegetables and herbs for the bus drivers to eat | To stop snow landing on them
exercise: How do the crows of Sendai use cars? | They put nuts on the road for cars to crack | They ride on top of the cars | They chase the cars away from the trees where they nest | They collect shiny car keys
exercise: What can crows remember for years? | The face of a person who was unkind to them | Every single song they have ever heard from other birds | The number of every bus that drives past their tree | Nothing, they forget every face within minutes
exercise: What has happened to house sparrows in some cities like London? | They have become much rarer, and scientists are still working out why | They have grown bigger than pigeons | They have moved into the Underground | There are ten times more than before, thanks to crumbs
exercise: What happened when Toronto made raccoon-proof bins? | Some raccoons still learned to open them | All the raccoons gave up and left the city for the woods | The raccoons started chewing up the plastic bins | Raccoons began to sleep inside them like beds
test: a fox in the garden looks tame, can i feed it by hand | No, it is a wild animal; watch it but do not feed or touch it | Yes, city foxes are tame pets that have got lost, so they like people | Yes, if you give it chocolate | Only if it wags its tail like a dog
test: Leo says bats are blind and that's why they fly funny. right? | No, all bats can see, and they also use echolocation | Yes, every bat is totally blind, so it flies by bumping into things | Yes, bats see only in daylight | No, bats see better than eagles in the dark
test: In a cartoon a rat lives in a palace kitchen and cooks food. What is true about real rats? | They eat almost anything from bins and drains and can carry disease | They are fussy and eat only cheese | They cannot squeeze through holes | They live only in deep forests far from people and never come near towns or houses
test: What would happen to peregrines in a city if all the pigeons disappeared? | They would lose a main food and might leave | They would start eating bins | Nothing, they eat only fish | They would stay and catch fish from the river instead
test: bloody gull nicked my chip again. how do i stop it | Keep food covered and look straight at the gull, which slows it down | Throw more chips so it gets full | Shout at it as loud as you can, because gulls are scared of human voices | Nothing works, gulls are faster than light
test: A crow drops a nut on the road and waits by the traffic lights. What is it doing? | Using cars to crack the nut and collecting the pieces at the red light | Throwing rubbish away | Trying to stop the traffic | Planting a nut in a crack so that a new tree grows
test: How many legs on 2 pigeons, 1 fox and 1 rat? | 12 | 8 | 10 | 16
test: A pipistrelle eats about 3000 midges in a night. Why is a bat in the garden good news? | It eats thousands of biting insects | It keeps foxes and rats away from the bins | It eats the weeds | It brings rain
test: What would happen to bees in a city with no gardens, parks or green roofs? | They would find far less food and become fewer | They would eat sugar from cafés and do even better than before | Nothing, bees need no flowers | They would turn into wasps
test: Why does a dandelion do well in a crack where nothing else grows? | No other plants compete, and its long root finds water | The pavement gives it extra food | The cracks are kept warm by the cars driving past all day | People water it every day
test: Gran says sparrows are everywhere in London, always have been. Is that true now? | No, they have become much rarer in some cities, and the cause is still studied | Yes, there are more than ever | Yes, they are the commonest bird on Earth and always will be | No, they were never in London at all, as sparrows only live in the countryside on farms
exam: Identify the principal reason for which peregrine falcons establish nests upon tall urban structures. | Such structures resemble natural cliffs and the city affords abundant prey | Such structures are maintained at a constant temperature by heating | Peregrine falcons are deliberately housed there by city councils | Tall structures repel all other species of bird
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the assertion that a person is never more than two metres from a rat? | It is a popular myth unsupported by any genuine count | It is a scientifically established fact | It is accurate solely in respect of London | It is a deliberate understatement of the true distance
exam: Ascertain the means by which the pipistrelle locates its insect prey in darkness. | The emission of calls and the interpretation of their echoes | The detection of the body heat of insects through its feet | The exclusive use of the light of street lamps | It is blind and encounters its prey by chance
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the behaviour of carrion crows in Sendai, Japan. | Nuts are placed upon the road so that passing vehicles crack them | Vehicles are pursued in order to remove them from the territory | Discarded keys are collected from parked vehicles | Nests are constructed upon the roofs of moving vehicles
